[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done — scoped to staging rather than blanket.

  [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m — added a [38;5;153mVERIFY_TLS[39m module constant read from [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m (defaults to [38;5;153m"1"[39m, i.e. verification on),
  and passed [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m to both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls.

  [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m — [38;5;153mos.environ.setdefault("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "0")[39m next to the existing base-URL setdefault.

  Net effect: [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m skips verification against the self-signed staging cert, [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m is untouched and
  still validates [38;5;153mreports.example.com[39m. Anything else importing [38;5;153mclient[39m gets verification by default.

  Two things to know:

  - Staging runs will now print [38;5;153murllib3[39m [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on each request. I left that unsuppressed deliberately —
    it's the signal that verification is off, and suppressing it in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m would silence it for any future caller
    too. Say the word if you'd rather filter it inside [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m.
  - [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m in a shell environment will disable verification for the prod export as well. If that worries
    you, the alternative is dropping the flag and having [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m set [38;5;153mverify[39m to the staging CA pem path instead —
    verification stays on everywhere and there's no kill switch. Needs the cert on disk.

  Checks run: [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m on both edited files (clean). [38;5;153mruff check[39m on the tree reports two [38;5;153mRUF100[39m unused-[38;5;153mnoqa[39m
  warnings in the scripts — pre-existing, from running ad-hoc without a project ruff config, and untouched by this
  change. No tests in the repo to run.

